fact WorkItem(id: "WI-392", description: some(value: "Typer: the unconstrained-type-param check must EXEMPT an operation's OWN type parameters when checking that operation's body. An op f[Elem,Eff](s: S[T=Elem,E=Eff]) -> ... = body whose body calls a type-parameterized op (incl. itself, e.g. Stream.collect/takeN -> splitFirst + recursion) on an arg of type S[T=Elem,...] makes the inner call's type param resolve to the enclosing op's OWN (legitimately-unbound, caller-instantiated) Global var; check_unconstrained_type_params (typing.rs:4214) wrongly flags it 'unconstrained'. FIX: thread the enclosing op's type-param vids from check_operation_bodies through TypingEnv into the check; a param resolving to one of them is CONSTRAINED. Use global logical vars, NOT Var::Rigid (bind_row_tail rejects rigid effect-row tails; rigid only unifies w/ itself, but Elem genuinely unifies w/ inner fresh params). Min repro: rewrap[A](b: Box[T=A]) -> Box[T=A] = idbox(b). Blocks WI-380."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-05T11:44:31Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-392", name: "typing")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-392", author: "user", content: "Design settled via dialogue 2026-06-05 (supersedes the ticket's initial 'exempt enclosing op params' framing). NOT rigid; use ordinary Global logical vars. ROOT CAUSE: extract_type_params (op_info.rs:240) returns STORED type-param vars (no per-call freshening). A SELF-recursive call (collect->collect, takeN->takeN) therefore reuses the ENCLOSING op's var -> ONE var, not two -> the no-op unify (?E =?= ?E) leaves NO substitution entry, and check_unconstrained_type_params (typing.rs:4214) walk_type_deep's it to the free ?E and flags 'unconstrained'. Cross-op calls (collect->splitFirst) already have distinct vars, so they don't alias (and splitFirst is a bodyless spec op dispatched separately, so it never hit this check) -- which is exactly why only collect/takeN errored. FIX (2 parts): (1) FRESHEN the callee's type params per call in check_apply_iter so the call-side var is DISTINCT from the enclosing op's (yields a real entry call_side := enclosing ?E); (2) the unconstrained check verifies the call-side param HAS A SUBSTITUTION ENTRY (subst.lookup(param).is_some() -- bound by THIS call) instead of walk_type_deep-to-ground. A genuine leak (empty[E]() with nothing to bind E) gets NO entry -> still flagged. RIGID rejected: bind_row_tail (typing.rs:8676, WI-336) refuses a Var::Rigid effect-row tail, so rigidifying the effect param Eff would break effects-row threading (Elem would be fine; Eff would not). Min repro: rewrap[A](b: Box[T=A]) -> Box[T=A] = idbox(b); plus a self-recursive drain[A](s: Box[T=A]) -> Box[T=A] = drain(s).", at: "2026-06-05T10:52:20Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-392", author: "user", content: "DESIGN CORRECTED 2026-06-05 (SUPERSEDES the prior 'freshen + check substitution-entry' note — that is UNSOUND). FINAL design = RIGID (Skolemize the op's OWN type params while checking its body). RATIONALE (soundness): checking collect[Elem,Eff]'s body means checking it for ALL Elem,Eff, so the body may CONSUME the param (it flows outward into the fresh call-side globals: rigid=>global, via resolved_var matching only Global so the flexible side binds TO the rigid) but must NEVER DETERMINE it (no global=>rigid: a rigid is never solved). Fresh-two-globals is UNSOUND: with both as Globals the link is bindable either way, so a body doing e.g. add(h,1) on h:Elem would unify Elem:=Int and typecheck under a forall-Elem signature. Rigid forbids that (Rigid(E)=?=Int fails). IMPL: in check_operation_bodies, before type_check_node, substitute each op type-param Global(vid)->Rigid(fresh) across op.params types + return_type + declared_effects (fresh rigid ids to avoid occurs-check). Then: the unconstrained check (typing.rs:4214) needs NO CHANGE (it flags only Global; callee globals resolve to rigids -> pass); NO callee freshening needed (callee flexible global solves to enclosing rigid). EFFECT-ROW caveat now mostly moot: bind_row_tail (typing.rs:8676) rejects a Rigid tail only when DECOMPOSING a row with present labels over the tail; collect/takeN bodies are pure so Eff is only unified as a bare row var (var fast-path, no decomposition). VERIFY no effectful body decomposes over a rigid tail; if one does, that is the WI-336 polymorphic-row handling (rejecting is sound meanwhile). Min repro still: rewrap[A](b:Box[T=A])->Box[T=A]=idbox(b) + self-recursive drain.", at: "2026-06-05T10:56:49Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-392", author: "user", content: "FINAL LOCKED DESIGN (consolidates the two notes above). (1) RIGID because DECLARED: collect[Elem,Eff] declares its params, so we are in CHECK mode -> Skolemize (rigidify) Elem,Eff while checking the body. If params were INFERRED from the body they'd have to be flexible (can't infer a rigid); since declared, rigid. Inner calls keep their OWN params FLEXIBLE (inferred from their args), solving TO the enclosing rigids (rigid=>global; resolved_var matches only Global so the flexible side binds). (2) ROWS CHECKED NOT BOUND: a rigid in an effect-row tail is CHECKED structurally (tail identity + present-label coverage), never solved -> bind_row_tail (the binding path, typing.rs:8676) is NOT on the checking path, so its rigid-tail rejection doesn't bite. collect/takeN pure -> tails match by identity, no extra labels. (3) NO CALLEE FRESHENING NEEDED given per-call Substitution::new() isolation (check_apply_iter typing.rs:2892) PLUS the invariant: no call's resolved output (return/effects/written-back type-args) may leave a SHARED STORED type-param var in the enclosing scope. Upheld by: pinned param resolves to ground-or-rigid (no stored var in output), un-pinned param caught by the unconstrained check (errors, doesn't leak). If that invariant is ever violated, ADD call-side freshening (instantiate the callee scheme with fresh vars per call) as the structural safeguard. IMPL: in check_operation_bodies, before type_check_node, substitute op type-param Global(vid)->Rigid(fresh) across op.params types + return_type + declared_effects. Unconstrained check (typing.rs:4214) needs NO change (passes non-Global). VERIFY: no effectful body decomposes a row over a rigid tail (else WI-336 territory); no resolved_ret leaks a stored type-param var. Min repro: rewrap[A](b:Box[T=A])->Box[T=A]=idbox(b) + self-recursive drain[A](s:Box[T=A])->Box[T=A]=drain(s).", at: "2026-06-05T11:05:41Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-392", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED a46ed9e. Two-part fix: (1) rigidify the op's OWN type params (Global->Rigid) in check_operation_bodies before the body check; (2) views_structurally_equal (term_view.rs) compares rigid/DeBruijn vars by full Var identity (was Opaque->unequal) so a rigidified effects-row param matches itself. VERIFIED: value/element param (rewrap, self-recursive drain) AND effect param (peel: the collect shape, Eff carried in Strm[E=Eff]) thread; soundness guard (ill-typed return stays rejected). Full anthill-core green (no regression; the views_structurally_equal branch only fires for rigid/DeBruijn, which the typer never produced pre-WI-392). CAVEAT for WI-380: bind_row_tail still rejects a Rigid effect-row TAIL when DECOMPOSING a row with present labels over it (subtype_effect_rows path) -- only bites a NON-PURE body that ADDS an effect label over the threaded rigid tail; collect/takeN default bodies are PURE so they don't hit it. If WI-380 adds effectful Stream ops with bodies, that path needs rigid-tail handling (WI-336 polymorphic-row territory).", at: "2026-06-05T11:44:31Z")
